# Earthquakes and Volcanoes: When the Earth Releases Energy

> Strain stored in rock and heat stored in the mantle escape as
> trembling ground and erupting mountains. This lesson explains how
> earthquakes are measured, where they cluster, and how volcanoes are
> built and classified.

## 1. Anatomy of an Earthquake

[definition]
An **earthquake** is the shaking of the ground caused by a sudden
release of accumulated strain along a fault. The rupture point at depth
is the **focus**; the point on the surface directly above it is the
**epicentre**, where shaking is usually strongest.
[/definition]

## 2. Seismic Waves

| Wave | Speed | Passes through | Character |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| P (primary) | Fastest | Solids, liquids, and gases | Push–pull; arrives first |
| S (secondary) | Slower | Solids only | Shearing; cannot cross the liquid core |
| L and Rayleigh (surface) | Slowest | Along the surface | Rolling ground motion; most destructive |

[info title="Waves as probes"]
The S-wave shadow zone — where no shear waves arrive — proved that the
outer core is liquid, a conclusion reached without drilling a single
metre into the Earth.
[/info]

## 3. Scales of Measurement

| Scale | Measures | Nature | Range |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Richter (magnitude) | Energy released | Objective, logarithmic | Open-ended; each whole step means about 10x ground motion |
| Mercalli (intensity) | Observed damage | Subjective, local | I (unnoticed) to XII (total destruction) |

[important]
One earthquake has one magnitude but many intensities: magnitude is
fixed by the energy released, while Mercalli intensity falls with
distance and depends on local building quality.
[/important]

## 4. Causes and Aftershocks

| Cause | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Tectonic (most quakes) | Movement along the San Andreas fault |
| Volcanic | Quakes accompanying eruptions |
| Collapse | Caving-in of mines and caverns |
| Human-induced | Reservoir loading (Koyna, Maharashtra, 1967) |

Small quakes that precede the main event are **foreshocks**; those that
follow are **aftershocks**, which can topple buildings already weakened
by the main shock.

## 5. Where Earthquakes and Volcanoes Cluster

- **Circum-Pacific Ring of Fire** — about 75% of the world's active
  volcanoes and roughly 90% of its large earthquakes.
- **Mid-Atlantic ridge** — shallow quakes along diverging plates.
- **Alpine–Himalayan belt** — quakes from the Africa–Eurasia and
  India–Eurasia collisions.

India's seismic zoning (Bureau of Indian Standards):

| Zone | Severity | Includes | Share of area |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| V | Most severe | NE India, Kashmir, Kutch, Andaman & Nicobar | ~11% |
| IV | Severe | Delhi, remaining Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal foothills | ~18% |
| III | Moderate | Most of the Ganga plain, parts of the peninsula | ~30% |
| II | Least | Interior of the Deccan plateau | ~41% |

## 6. Volcano Anatomy and Types

@diagram tree id="volcano-anatomy" layout="tree" aria="Parts of a volcano from magma chamber to crater"
  Volcano -> "Magma chamber"
  Volcano -> "Conduit and vent"
  Volcano -> "Crater"
  Volcano -> "Lava flows"
  Volcano -> "Ash and pyroclasts"
@/diagram

| Type | Behaviour | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shield | Fluid basaltic lava, gentle slopes | Mauna Loa, Hawaii |
| Composite (stratovolcano) | Alternating lava and ash layers; explosive | Fuji, Vesuvius, Stromboli |
| Cinder cone | Small, steep cone of loose fragments | Paricutin, Mexico |
| Caldera | Summit collapses after a huge eruption | Krakatoa, Toba |
| Fissure eruption | Lava floods from long cracks | Deccan Traps; Iceland |

## 7. Active, Dormant, and Extinct

| Class | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Active | Erupts regularly or recently | Barren Island — India's only active volcano |
| Dormant | Sleeping between long intervals | Vesuvius, which buried Pompeii in 79 CE |
| Extinct | No eruption in recorded history; deeply eroded | Most Deccan Trap vents |

## 8. Hot Springs and Geysers

Groundwater heated by shallow magma emerges as **hot springs**; where
superheated water flashes periodically to steam, a **geyser** erupts.
Old Faithful at Yellowstone is the famous example, and the word geyser
itself comes from Iceland's Great Geysir. In India, Manikaran (Himachal
Pradesh) and the Puga valley (Ladakh) are notable geothermal springs.

[note]
Geothermal fields like Puga are being studied as clean-energy sources
that tap the same heat that powers volcanoes.
[/note]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Fastest seismic wave? | P-wave (through all three states) |
| Most destructive waves? | Surface (L and Rayleigh) waves |
| Richter measures? | Magnitude — energy, logarithmically |
| Mercalli measures? | Observed intensity, I to XII |
| Ring of Fire holds? | ~75% of volcanoes, ~90% of large quakes |
| India's Zone V covers? | NE India, Kashmir, Kutch, Andaman & Nicobar |
| India's only active volcano? | Barren Island (Andaman Sea) |
